{
  "id": "en.verbs.modals-can-must",
  "language": "en",
  "category": "verbs",
  "title": "Modals: can, must",
  "summary": "Talking about ability and obligation.",
  "cefrLevel": "A2",
  "prerequisites": [],
  "theory": [
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "markdown": "Modal verbs sit between the subject and the main verb. The main verb stays in **base form** (no -s, no -ing).\n\n- **can** = ability (\"be able to\"). *I can swim.*\n- **must** = strong obligation. *You must wear a helmet.*\n- **can't** = inability/prohibition. *He can't drive.*\n- **mustn't** = prohibition. *You mustn't smoke here.*\n\nNo *do/does* in negatives or questions: *Can you swim? — Yes, I can.*"
    },
    {
      "kind": "examples",
      "items": [
        { "source": "She can speak three languages.", "translation": "Mówi trzema językami." },
        { "source": "You must finish your homework.", "translation": "Musisz skończyć zadanie domowe." },
        { "source": "Can you help me?", "translation": "Możesz mi pomóc?" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "type": "fill",
      "prompt": "I ___ (be able / swim) very well.",
      "answers": ["can swim"]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "She ___ speak French.",
      "options": ["cans", "can", "can to", "is can"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "type": "fill",
      "prompt": "You ___ (obligation / wear) a helmet here.",
      "answers": ["must wear"]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "We ___ to leave now.",
      "options": ["must", "must to", "musts", "are must"],
      "answer": 0
    }
  ]
}
